  "success": true,
  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Canon (`web/`)\n\nI read through the actual route handlers, server queries, and client components rather than guessing. Headline: this codebase is unusually disciplined about the classic pitfalls — the N+1 and caching categories are mostly already solved, with comments in the code explaining why. The real, concrete findings are concentrated in React re-renders and two missed caching opportunities on immutable data. I did not find any memory leaks.\n\n---\n\n### 1. N+1 query patterns — none found (already deliberately avoided)\n\n`web/lib/server/conflicts.ts:172-200` (`getQueuePage`) and `web/lib/server/export.ts` batch everything: one joined query for the page of rows, one grouped query for child counts across the *whole page* (`countChildrenByParent`), and counts via `Promise.all`. The comment at `conflicts.ts:169` says it directly: *\"Three round trips, not three hundred... A reviewer working 400 conflicts should not be paying for a query per row.\"* This is correct and I have nothing to add here.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Unnecessary React re-renders — real issue in the conflict queue\n\n`components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx:172-189` (ledger view) and `conflict-dense-table.tsx` re-render **every row in the list** on every `j`/`k` keypress, even though only the previously-selected and newly-selected row actually changed visually.\n\nTwo compounding causes:\n\n**a) A fresh closure per row, per render, breaks any memoization:**\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx:180\n<ConflictDiffRow\n  ...\n  submit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}   // new function identity every render\n```\n`submitFor` is stable (`useCallback`), but *calling* it inside `.map` allocates a new arrow function every time the parent re-renders — which happens on every `setSelectedIndex` call.\n\n**b) No `React.memo` on the row component**, so even with a stable `submit` prop, all rows would still re-render because `ConflictDiffRow`/`ConflictDenseTable`'s row button isn't memoized.\n\nAt the 100-row page size (`QUEUE_PAGE_SIZE`), that's up to 100 `ConflictSides`/`DiffValue` trees (each running `diffSegments`, see #4) re-rendering per keystroke, in a UI whose whole design goal is \"a reviewer working a queue of 400 should never touch the mouse.\"\n\n**Fix** — memoize the row and give it a stable per-row callback via a `Map` cache or `id`-parameterized callback pattern instead of a factory call in the render path:\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-queue.tsx\nconst ConflictDiffRow = memo(ConflictDiffRowImpl); // wrap the export\n\n// pass conflictId + a stable dispatcher instead of a bound closure\n<ConflictDiffRow\n  key={row.conflictId}\n  row={row}\n  conflictId={row.conflictId}\n  onSubmit={submit}          // stable useCallback, takes (conflictId, action)\n  selected={index === clamped}\n  onSelect={onSelectIndex}    // stable useCallback(index => setSelectedIndex(index))\n  ...\n/>\n```\nand inside the row, call `onSubmit(conflictId, action)` directly rather than receiving a pre-bound function. Combined with `React.memo`, a `j`/`k` press then only re-renders the two rows whose `selected` prop actually flipped.\n\nEverywhere else in the app is Server Components or narrowly-scoped client leaves (`run-live.tsx`, `logo-loop.tsx`, `gradient-waves.tsx`) — those are fine; this queue is the one place worth fixing.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Caching opportunities\n\n**a) `getRuleset(id)` — reads an immutable row on every queue page load.**\n`web/lib/server/rulesets.ts:44-49` and the comment on the file itself: *\"Rulesets are VERSIONED, NEVER EDITED IN PLACE... There is deliberately no update path in this file.\"* A ruleset row, once created, never changes. Yet `loadQueue` (`conflicts.ts:219-230`) re-fetches it from Postgres on every request. This is a clean candidate for Next's `unstable_cache` keyed by id, with no revalidation needed (a new version gets a new row, not a mutation of the old one):\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/rulesets.ts\nimport { unstable_cache } from \"next/cache\";\n\nexport const getRuleset = unstable_cache(\n  async (id: string): Promise<Ruleset | null> => {\n    const [row] = await getDb().select().from(rulesets).where(eq(rulesets.id, id)).limit(1);\n    return row ? rulesetSchema.parse(row) : null;\n  },\n  [\"ruleset-by-id\"],\n  { revalidate: false }, // immutable once written — never needs to expire\n);\n```\n\n**b) `getRunDetail` for terminal runs — same shape of opportunity.**\nOnce `run.status` is `\"complete\"` or `\"failed\"`, the row (`runs.ts:90-103`) never changes again, but it's re-read on every navigation to `/conflicts`, `/audit?runId=`, and the export page for that run. Since freshness only matters for in-flight runs (already handled separately by the no-store polling in `run-live.tsx`), a `unstable_cache` wrapper keyed by `runId`, invalidated via `revalidateTag` from wherever the engine/web writes a terminal status, would remove a repeated round trip for every screen a reviewer visits about a finished run.\n\nI'd hold off on caching `getQueuePage` itself — filters and pagination make the key space large, and a reviewer approving conflicts needs to see their own writes immediately (the queue is intentionally read-your-writes fresh via `router.refresh()`).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Redundant computations\n\n**a) `diffSegments` recomputed on every re-render, compounding with #2.**\n`components/conflicts/diff-value.tsx:25` calls `diffSegments(value, other)` — an O(n) prefix/suffix scan — inside render, for every value, on every row, on every re-render. It's cheap per call, but because of the re-render bug in #2, it currently runs ~2× the visible rows × values-per-row on every keystroke instead of only for the 2 rows that changed. Fixing #2 removes essentially all of this waste for free; no separate memoization of `diffSegments` itself is needed once rows stop needlessly re-rendering.\n\n**b) `RulesetEditor`'s row lookup is O(n²), harmless at current scale.**\n`components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:166-167`:\n```tsx\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);          // O(n) per row\n  return (\n    <RuleRow position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1} ... />  // another O(n)\n```\n`orderRulesByEvaluation(rules)` also recomputes on every keystroke in any field. For a ruleset of a few dozen rules this is genuinely negligible (not worth adding `useMemo`/index-map complexity for a table this size) — flagging only so it doesn't get copied into a screen with a larger list later. If it ever needs fixing, build an `id → index` map once per render instead of calling `.indexOf` per row.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Memory leaks — none found\n\nChecked every `useEffect` with a timer, listener, or WebGL/ResizeObserver in the client tree:\n- `run-live.tsx` (`Elapsed`) — clears its `setInterval` on unmount and on `running` flip. Correct.\n- `conflict-queue.tsx` — `keydown` listener removed in the same effect's cleanup. Correct.\n- `gradient-waves.tsx` — the most complex effect in the app (WebGL renderer, `ResizeObserver`, `requestAnimationFrame` loop) — cancels the frame, disconnects the observer, removes the context-lost listener, removes the canvas, and explicitly calls `loseContext()` on teardown. This is more thorough than most production code.\n- `logo-loop.tsx` — `ResizeObserver` disconnected on cleanup. Correct.\n- TanStack Query is scoped to a single leaf (`QueryProvider` mounted only inside `RunLive`, not the root layout — per `query-provider.tsx`'s own comment), so there's no unbounded client-side cache growth across navigation.\n\nNothing to fix here.\n\n---\n\n### Summary of action items, in priority order\n\n| # | File | Fix | Effort |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `components/conflicts/conflict-queue.tsx`, `conflict-diff-row.tsx`, `conflict-dense-table.tsx` | Stop allocating per-row closures in render; wrap row components in `React.memo` | Medium — touches the review-critical path, test keyboard nav after |\n| 2 | `lib/server/rulesets.ts` | Wrap `getRuleset` in `unstable_cache`, no revalidation | Small |\n| 3 | `lib/server/runs.ts` | Wrap `getRunDetail` in `unstable_cache`, revalidate-by-tag on terminal status write | Small–medium (need to find/add the invalidation call site on the engine-status-write path) |\n\nNone of these change detection or resolution logic, so no eval harness re-run is needed. Per AGENTS.md, #2/#3 add `unstable_cache` — that's a Next.js API already in the dependency surface, not a new library, so no permission needed; happy to implement any of these if you want to proceed.\n",
